>> title: Panzanella
>> category: Salads
>> tags: bread, tomato, basil, tuscan, no-cook
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: panzanella, panzanela, tuscan bread salad, italian bread salad, bread and tomato salad, stale bread salad
>> pairs-with: fattoush, ciabatta
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: narrow — there is a window where the bread is soaked through and still has a spine, and it is about twenty minutes wide
>> step.1: salt 30 min, keep the juice
>> step.2: fry 6 min in the oil
>> step.3: macerate 20 min
>> step.4: whisk into the tomato water
>> step.5: throw in, stand 10 min

Salt @ripe tomatoes{1 1/2%lb}(700 g; cut in rough chunks) with @kosher salt{3/4%tsp} in a #bowl{} for ~stand{30%min}. Everything below depends on this: the salad is dressed with what comes out of the tomatoes, so a pale winter tomato gives you a dry salad no amount of oil fixes.

Fry @stale country bread{6%cups}(torn in rough pieces, crusts on) in @extra-virgin olive oil{1/4%cup}(60 mL) in a wide #skillet{} over medium heat for ~fry{6%min}, turning until the edges are gold and the middles are still soft. Gold edges and a soft middle is the exact texture the salad needs — all-crisp bread never softens evenly, and untoasted bread turns to paste.

Macerate @red onion{1/2%small}(sliced paper thin) in @red wine vinegar{2%Tbs}(30 mL) for ~stand{20%min}.

Whisk @extra-virgin olive oil{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @garlic{1%clove}(grated) into @&(~3)salted tomatoes{} and all their juice. There is no dressing recipe to reach for here and there could not be one: the tomato water is the dressing, and it does not exist until the tomatoes have been sitting in salt.

Throw @&(~3)fried bread{} into @&(~1)dressed tomatoes{} with @&(~2)macerated onion{}, @basil{1%cup}(torn) and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}, and let it ~stand{10%min} before it goes to the table.

Ten minutes for the bread to drink. Past about forty this one is porridge.
